  Share
  
  You’re on a mission as an **Agile coach** or **Scrum Master** to **inspire** your **team** and make **incredible** things happen. But suddenly, you find yourself when **Agile** and **Scrum** feel like **buzzwords; productivity plunges,** andthe team **puzzles** seem unsolvable. **Overwhelming**.
  
  Trust me; I’ve been in your exact **spot**, faced the same **challenges**, and **emerged victorious.** And now, I’m here to share my **personal experiences** and **offer** a **helping** hand to **guide** you back onto the **path to success**.
  
  ## The Initial Challenges: A Personal Anecdote
  
  My tenure as a **Scrum Master** started on a challenging note on my first day at work. My business owner-manager questioned, **_“Does your Agile have planning, a roadmap or something?!”_** A sense of urgency swept over me as I recognized the hurdles ahead.
  
  During those initial weeks, I encountered **communication gaps**, **conflicting user stories**, **low productivity,** and **unpredictable value deliveries, or was it value?** Our backlog was filled with **spikes** and **incomplete** **stories** **carried** **over** **from** **one sprint to another**. The lack of **empowerment** and **motivation** was evident; our **daily stand-ups** felt like a **status** **call** without a clear idea and understanding of the **stage of the work** items!Moreover, most of my team members, being **remote** and **introverted** individuals, operated in **isolated silos** with **no shared goal and vision** among **multiple vendors.** And,Oh, and let’s not forget the additional hurdle of dealing with some **turnover**. It was a **whirlwind**, to say the least.
  
  ## The Transformation Journey: A Path of Continuous Improvement
  
  As a **seasoned coach**, I understand that transforming the team requires more than just **motivational speeches** and **pep talks**. Let’s face it; the journey isn’t always smooth. Yet, through months of **hard work**, **unwavering** **commitment**, and a **willingness to embrace** **vulnerability**, we set sail toward **success.** Our approach is **rooted in progression**, **not** **perfection**, as we **continuously** **strive** to **better ourselves** and our **processes**. I have witnessed my team **overcome** **obstacles** and reach their **higher potential,** a journey that continues today. This experience affirms the power of **true leadership.** It **reinforced** my belief in **leading** with a generous dose of **empathy**, **dedication**, **resilience**, and a dash of **passion,** true to the spirit of the **Kaizen** approach.
  
  We made **continuous** **improvements** as our **North Star**, constantly striving to better ourselves day by day. **_Challenges were not roadblocks but invitations to adventure._** To our surprise, we started **achieving** **feats** that seemed **out of reach**. This culture of **relentless** **improvement** became the cornerstone of our success.
  
  In this article, I’ll walk you through a five-step approach to transforming your team into a **high-performing Agile team** with a [**Kaizen approach**](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaizen) while also discussing the role of **vulnerability in leadership,** the importance of **emotional intelligence**, and how to create a **safe space** for your team to be open and honest. Let’s dive in and make sure to have some fun along the way; after all, we’re all human, right?
  
  ## **_Step 1: Understanding the current situation_**
  
  It’s essential to **assess your team’s current level of Agile maturity before** **starting** on any **transformational** **journey**. This is **not** just **ticking the boxes**; the step involves **identifying** the **negative patterns/ anti-patterns** preventing your team from **achieving optimal performance**. The **assessment** provides a **baseline** of the **team’s** **strengths** and **weaknesses** and helps you **identify** areas that need **improvement**.
  
  There are various standards and some free assessments tools available to assess your **team’s Agile maturity**, including the **Agile Maturity Model**, **Scrum Maturity Assessment**, **Team Agility Assessment**, **Agile Fluency Diagnostic**, **Innovation Maturity Model**, **LACE Competency Assessment**, **SAFe Agile Team Self-Assessment**, and **Agile Self-Assessment Game**. These assessments provide **insights** into the **team’s Agile practices**, **processes**, and **culture** and help **identify areas** for **improvement**.
  
